    "narrative": "The focus for LawDog today centers on high-upside offensive environments, specifically targeting the matchup against Bradford (L). **Zack Gelof** (67.85) stands as our premier play; he benefits from an elite 19% boost in SP skill projection against the starter and a massive +10% park factor, cementing his status as a must-start. He is flanked by **Brendan Donovan** (66.83), who leverages a favorable leadoff spot and a 14% platoon edge to anchor the top of our scoring. **Bryce Harper** (63.28) and **Lawrence Butler** (63.24) round out our top-tier tier, with **Bryce Harper** maintaining strong output despite a neutral pitcher matchup, while **Lawrence Butler** overcomes a negative platoon split purely on the strength of his park-adjusted efficiency.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Daulton Varsho** (52.65). Despite a 10% platoon advantage against Woo (R), his efficiency score ultimately fell short of our outfield and utility locks. We prioritized **Dylan Beavers** (58.02) over **Daulton Varsho** primarily due to the unique situational advantage provided by a slow pitcher delivery, which elevates **Dylan Beavers**\u2019 stolen base potential\u2014a critical factor given the scarcity of speed in our league. While **Daulton Varsho** brings reliable plate discipline, the optimizer prioritized the specific situational upside of **Dylan Beavers** to maximize our 5x5 category contributions.\n\nManagers should monitor the lineup for **Cal Raleigh** (54.86), who carries some risk today; he is currently batting seventh, and while his recency bias is strong, the -6% dynamic platoon penalty against Brown (R) limits his ceiling. Similarly, **Yainer Diaz** (44.94) remains a marginal play at the bottom of the order against Woo (R). We are effectively sacrificing higher-ceiling options like **Jonathan Aranda** and **JJ Bleday** to keep our game cap flexibility intact, but the core remains well-positioned to capitalize on the favorable park factors for our primary hitters.",
